      "honestResidual": "The statement COUNT is still higher than the parent (37-38 vs 18): the scan genuinely makes more round trips because it has to reject 5990 rows before it can answer, and the parent answered by reading everything once. What changed is the cost per statement -- bridge bytes fall 97.1% (29.2MB -> 853KB) and p50 falls 58% (186ms -> 78ms), so the extra round trips are much cheaper than the one big read they replace. The remaining tail cost is inherent to post-authorization paging with this visibility distribution; bounding the probe further would require restricting the candidate projection again or capping the scan, which is a separate decision."
